--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The Broken Lantern Inn creaked under the morning chill of Elaris Hollow, its timber beams sighing as if tired of holding up another day. Sunlight, thin and pale, slipped through the slats of the shutters, catching dust motes dancing above the narrow bed where you stirred. The wool blanket scratched against your skin, heavy with the scent of smoke and damp earth. On the nightstand, a clay cup sat half-filled with cold tea, its rim chipped, the liquid still faintly steaming—as if someone had just set it there.

You reached for it—slow, half-asleep—and the cup slid two inches across the table, untouched. You froze. Looked around. Nothing moved. The cup rested again, ordinary, inert. You picked it up. It was warm. Just warm. You drank. The tea tasted of mint and something sharper—like iron, maybe. Or rain on stone.

Outside, the market woke. A cart wheel cracked over cobblestone. A woman shouted about fresh eggs. A dog yapped, then fell silent. Then—*clatter*. Something heavy dropped. Someone laughed. A bell rang once, clear and low, from the square. You didn’t move. Didn’t need to. You already knew what came next.

You set down the cup. The door stood open. Wind curled inside. You could walk out. Walk toward the well. Toward the forest edge. Or stay. Wait. See what else might slide on its own.

What do you do?

