--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The mist clung to your boots as you stepped past the inn’s crooked sign—the lantern, now dark, swayed slightly, though there was no wind. Beyond the threshold, the square unfolded in quiet disarray: baskets overturned, a spilled sack of dried herbs releasing their last breath of lavender and thyme, and the well—old, moss-slicked, its stone lips worn smooth by centuries of hands.

He was there. Callum. His coat was dusty, his fingers trembling as he gripped the edge of his broken cartwheel. When he saw you, he flinched—not in fear, but recognition. Like he’d seen you somewhere. Sometime. Maybe in a dream.

“You’re… you’re the one,” he whispered. “The one who walked out.”

He swallowed hard, eyes darting to the trees beyond the square, where shadows stretched too long even in daylight.

“My box,” he choked. “It’s just… just a few vials. Rosewater from the high meadows. Honeycomb dipped in moonpetal wax. Nothing worth stealing. But they took the *map* inside.” He pulled a torn corner of parchment from his pocket—charred at the edges, ink bleeding into strange glyphs that shifted when you looked away.

“They left this behind. Said… said ‘Tell the Fool he owes us a favor.”

He handed it to you. The paper was warm. And humming.

Behind him, the well water rippled—though no breeze touched it. And for just a heartbeat, you swear—you saw a shadow standing in the deep, watching. Watching you.

Then it vanished.

Callum blinked. “I—I’m sorry. I shouldn’t be saying any of this. I don’t know why I told you…”

But you were already walking toward the Whispering Forest Edge.

Because you remembered.

The raven hadn’t lied.

The Fool doesn’t give tasks.

He gives invitations.

And you… you never learned to say no.

