The raven doesn’t answer.

It simply lifts one claw—slow, deliberate—and scratches a single line across the ground between you and the well.

The dirt parts.

Not with wind. Not with magic you can name.

With *memory*.

A voice rises—not from the bird, not from the air—but from the stones beneath your feet. Ancient. Quiet. Familiar.

*"You walked out…*

…when the lantern died.  
…when the tea went cold.  
…when no one called your name.

You didn’t choose the title.  
You earned it by refusing to wait.

The Fool isn’t a role.  
It’s a refusal.

To bow. To kneel. To ask permission.

You didn't come here looking for answers.

You came because you stopped believing anyone would give them to you.

So now you go.

Not because you’re brave.

Because you’re done waiting.

The map in your hand trembles—not from fear.

From recognition.

You look back at Callum.

His eyes are wide.

He sees it too.

The mark on your palm.

The one that wasn’t there yesterday.

The one shaped like a question mark.

He stammers, voice cracking.

“I—I thought it was just a myth. A story mothers tell to keep children from wandering. But… you’re real.”

You nod.

No explanation.

Just a step forward.

The raven launches into the sky—and dissolves into a thousand black petals, each one bearing a different face. All of them yours.

And below, the well bubbles—not with water.

But with voices.

Whispering.

One word.

Again.

Again.

Again.

***Fool.**

***

