Callum’s voice cracks like dry kindling.

“Oh gods—oh gods, no. Not you. Not *here*. The mark—it’s real. It’s *you*.”

He stumbles back, heel catching on a loose cobble. He doesn’t fall. Just freezes. Stares at your palm. At the question mark glowing faintly in the dim light, pulsing like a slow heartbeat. His breath hitches. Tears well, hot and sudden, spilling down his cheeks. He doesn’t wipe them away.

“The stories…” he whispers, voice breaking into sobs. “They said the Fool wouldn’t come asking. Wouldn’t beg. Wouldn’t plead. They said he’d just… show up. Silent. Unannounced. And when he did… the world forgot why it feared him.”

He looks up, eyes raw.

“And now you stand here. And the well whispers. And the raven flies. And my cart is broken. My box is gone. And I’m just… just a man with a map I can’t read. And you—you’re the thing I can’t unsee.”

He drops to his knees.

Not in prayer.

In surrender.

The well gurgles.

The raven tilts its head.

And from the dark water, one name rises.

Not in sound.

In *feeling*.

A name you knew once.

Before you forgot.

Before you chose to walk out.

The name echoes—

*…Liora…*

Callum gasps.

“No,” he cries. “That’s impossible. That’s the name of the last Fool’s Bride. She vanished ten years ago. She was *your* sister. You were the boy who ran. Who disappeared. Who became… *nothing*.”

Silence.

Even the wind holds its breath.

You don’t remember her.

But your hands remember.

They’re shaking.

Not from fear.

From *recognition*.

The map in your grip grows warmer.

The glyphs shift.

Now they spell not directions.

But a date.

And a place.

Beneath the forest’s roots.

Where the first well was dug.

By a girl with golden hair.

Who held a lantern.

And sang.

Until she didn’t.

Callum looks up.

Tears still streak his face.

He doesn’t ask.

He doesn’t need to.

He knows.

He bows his head.

And whispers:

“Forgive me, Liora.”

The well goes still.

The raven lets out a single, soft coo.

And the path ahead…

…opens.

